+I couple a closed, fixed-composition gas and Keller--Miksis mechanics to conservative radial heat transport in material water volumes and dynamic gold nanoparticle storage. One signed current-state gas--liquid flux closes both energy equations; no precomputed transfer efficiency enters. A 10 ns, 10 J/m$^2$ pulse absorbed by one 20 nm-radius Au particle under prescribed near-wall confinement raises the computed five-cycle maximum by about 1.41 and 2.18 microkelvin at 1.0 and 1.15 bar. The former is a conditional reduced-model result; the latter is a formal fixed-composition scenario, despite completing five numerical cycles. Feedback respectively increases and reduces the one-way effect. The wet 1.3 bar trajectory is coefficient-censored; dry formal variants retain higher temperatures. Spherical source averaging substantially underpredicts local Au heating. Numerical completion does not establish a validated physical gas state, realizable nanoparticle environment or fusion condition.
